    "reply": "The gene NCBIGene:1080, named CF transmembrane conductance regulator, is associated with cystic fibrosis [1][2]. Two additional genes retrieved in the same query are Fc gamma receptor IIa (NCBIGene:2212) and transforming growth factor beta 1 (NCBIGene:7040) [3][4].\n\n## Disease Context\n\nCystic fibrosis is represented in MedGen under that exact name and is also indexed in the literature as Cystic Fibrosis [6][7]. The MedGen record lists multiple clinical features, including asthma, bronchiectasis, clubbing of fingers, dehydration, diarrhea, hemoptysis, hepatomegaly, and hepatosplenomegaly [11][15][19][23][27][28][29][30].\n\n## Liver and Neurologic Complications\n\nA recent publication describes cystic fibrosis liver disease as affecting a large proportion of CF patients, noting that encephalopathy is a rare complication that can be precipitated by triggers such as systemic corticosteroids in previously stable disease [26: \"Cystic fibrosis liver disease (CFLD) affects a large proportion of cystic fibrosis (CF) patients; however encephalopathy is a rare complication\"][26: \"hyperammonemic encephalopathy can be precipitated in previously stable CFLD by various triggers including systemic corticosteroids\"]. This case report corresponds to the PubMed title Encephalopathy in cystic fibrosis [5].\n\n## Clinical Trials and Literature\n\nSeveral registered clinical trials target cystic fibrosis, including assessments of quadriceps muscle electrostimulation and a study of pregnancies in CF patients [8][24]. Additional trials indexed under fibrocystic breast disease are distinct from cystic fibrosis [12][16][20]. Recent PubMed-indexed editorials cover nutrition, pediatric cystic fibrosis, cystic fibrosis-related diabetes, and azithromycin use in CF [10][14][18][22].",
    "dur": 2.753
   },
