    "openrouter_cost_usd": 0.004757,
    "reply": "Three genes are associated with cystic fibrosis in the retrieved records: NCBIGene:1080 (CF transmembrane conductance regulator), NCBIGene:2212 (Fc gamma receptor IIa), and NCBIGene:7040 (transforming growth factor beta 1) [1][2][3][4]. The disease is identified as Cystic fibrosis [2], also indexed as Cystic Fibrosis in MedGen and literature entity records [6][7].\n\n## Core Gene Associations\n\nNCBIGene:1080 encodes the CF transmembrane conductance regulator, the principal gene linked to Cystic fibrosis [1][2]. Two additional genes appear in the association set: NCBIGene:2212, named Fc gamma receptor IIa, and NCBIGene:7040, named transforming growth factor beta 1 [3][4].\n\n## Clinical Features of Cystic Fibrosis\n\nMedGen records list multiple clinical features for this condition, including Asthma, Bronchiectasis, Clubbing of fingers, Dehydration, Diarrhea, Hemoptysis, Hepatomegaly, and Hepatosplenomegaly [11][15][19][23][27][28][29][30].\n\n## Supporting Literature and Trials\n\nPubMed records cover topics such as Encephalopathy in cystic fibrosis, Nutrition in Cystic Fibrosis, Cystic Fibrosis in Children, Azithromycin and cystic fibrosis, and Cystic fibrosis-related diabetes, with PMIDs 32800451, 35240644, 35592838, 35853636, and 39129918 respectively [5][9][10][13][14][17][18][21][22][25]. One abstract describes hyperammonemic encephalopathy as a rare complication precipitated by triggers including systemic corticosteroids in cystic fibrosis liver disease [26: \"hyperammonemic encephalopathy\" can be precipitated in previously stable CFLD by various triggers including systemic corticosteroids\"]. Clinical trials include quadriceps muscle electrostimulation in cystic fibrosis patients and a pregnancies-and-cystic-fibrosis study [8][24], alongside three trials addressing fibrocystic breast disease rather than cystic fibrosis itself [12][16][20].",
    "dur": 3.241
   }
